if (typeof ARTICLES !== 'undefined') {
  var ENRICH = {
    "isle-of-skye-motorcycle-guide": {
      updatedDate: "2026-07-21",
      keyTakeaways: [
        "Reach Skye by the toll-free Skye Bridge at Kyle of Lochalsh, or take the seasonal six-car Glenelg-Kylerhea ferry (Easter to October) for a slower, wildlife-rich crossing.",
        "The best roads include the B8083 dead-end to Elgol, the A855 Trotternish Loop past the Old Man of Storr and Quiraing, and the B8009 to Neist Point lighthouse.",
        "Portree is the main town for hotels, fuel and food; Broadford in the south has fuel and a supermarket, with Armadale a third fuel option.",
        "Weather is notoriously changeable; May and June offer the longest, driest days, while July and August bring midges.",
        "Ride single-track roads before 9am to beat campervans and tour buses, and always pull into passing places on your left."
      ],
      comparisonTable: {
        caption: "Must-see Skye stops and why to visit",
        headers: ["Stop", "Why Visit", "Notes"],
        rows: [
          ["Quiraing (Trotternish)", "Surreal landslip of pinnacles and hidden valleys", "Skye's most photographed landscape, on the A855"],
          ["Old Man of Storr", "Iconic rocky pinnacle visible from the road", "Popular walk, busy car park in summer"],
          ["Fairy Pools, Glen Brittle", "Crystal-clear waterfalls from the Cuillins", "Magical in any weather"],
          ["Elgol (B8083)", "Finest view in Scotland across Loch Scavaig", "Dead-end road, retrace your route"],
          ["Neist Point (B8009)", "Westernmost point with lighthouse and cliffs", "Dramatic sunset spot"]
        ]
      },
      faq: [
        { q: "How do I get to the Isle of Skye by motorcycle?", a: "<p>The simplest route is the toll-free Skye Bridge at Kyle of Lochalsh, open year-round. For something more memorable, take the seasonal Glenelg-Kylerhea ferry, a tiny vessel carrying just six cars that runs from Easter to October. The crossing passes through waters where otters and seals are common, and it makes a worthwhile detour.</p>" },
        { q: "When is the best time to visit Skye?", a: "<p>May and June bring the longest days and usually the driest weather, making them ideal for riding. July and August are warm but midges can be fierce, so carry repellent. September and October deliver autumn colours and often settled conditions. Skye's weather is changeable year-round, so pack waterproofs whatever the season.</p>" },
        { q: "Where can I get fuel on Skye?", a: "<p>Fuel is available in Portree, Broadford and Armadale. Beyond these three points the island has no reliable fuel, so fill up before heading to remote spots like Elgol or Neist Point. Never leave fuel to chance on the single-track dead-end roads.</p>" },
        { q: "Where should I stay and eat on Skye?", a: "<p>Portree is the main hub with hotels, restaurants and services, while Broadford in the south is quieter with a supermarket. Wild camping is permitted under Scottish right-to-roam laws. For a splurge, the Three Chimneys restaurant near Dunvegan is world-class.</p>" },
        { q: "Are Skye's roads difficult to ride?", a: "<p>Skye's northern and western roads are predominantly single-track with passing places, while the south has wider A-roads such as the A87 from the bridge to Portree. Pull into passing places on your left, never park in them, and watch for sheep wandering onto the road, especially in spring.</p>" }
      ]
    },
    "glencoe-fort-william-guide": {
      updatedDate: "2026-07-21",
      keyTakeaways: [
        "The A82 through Glen Coe is regularly voted Britain's most scenic road, flanked by the Three Sisters and the Aonach Eagach ridge.",
        "Fort William sits at the foot of Ben Nevis and offers full facilities, making it an ideal touring base for Lochaber.",
        "Detour up the single-track Glen Etive road (the Skyfall setting) and ride the A830 Road to the Isles to Mallaig via the Glenfinnan Viaduct.",
        "The Clachaig Inn in Glen Coe is a legendary bikers' pub, while the Alexandra Hotel in Fort William offers secure parking.",
        "Glen Coe is among the wettest places in Britain, so pack waterproofs and expect moody, atmospheric conditions."
      ],
      comparisonTable: {
        caption: "Rides from the Glencoe and Fort William base",
        headers: ["Route", "Highlight", "Notes"],
        rows: [
          ["A82 through Glen Coe", "Britain's most dramatic valley", "Ride at dawn or dusk for empty roads"],
          ["A830 Road to the Isles", "Glenfinnan Viaduct and coast to Mallaig", "The Harry Potter bridge"],
          ["Glen Etive single-track", "12 miles to a remote loch head", "The Skyfall filming location"],
          ["Glen Nevis dead-end", "Wild road beneath Ben Nevis", "Atmospheric, retrace your route"],
          ["Corran Ferry to Ardnamurchan", "Britain's most westerly mainland point", "Short crossing from near Fort William"]
        ]
      },
      faq: [
        { q: "Where should I base myself in the area?", a: "<p>Fort William is the natural base, sitting at the foot of Ben Nevis with fuel, supermarkets, outdoor shops and accommodation at every price point. The Alexandra Hotel in the town centre is biker-friendly with secure parking. For something more atmospheric, the Clachaig Inn in Glen Coe itself is a legendary walkers' and bikers' pub with real ales and bar meals.</p>" },
        { q: "What is the best way to experience Glen Coe?", a: "<p>Ride the A82 through the glen at dawn or dusk when the light transforms the valley and you will likely have the road to yourself. The Three Sisters loom on the south side and the Buachaille Etive Mor guards the eastern entrance. For more, branch onto the single-track Glen Etive road, which featured in the Bond film Skyfall.</p>" },
        { q: "Where can I get fuel and servicing?", a: "<p>Fuel is readily available in Fort William and Ballachulish. The nearest motorcycle dealers are in Inverness, roughly 65 miles north, so carry basic tools and a tyre repair kit for the more remote roads around Lochaber.</p>" },
        { q: "What is the weather like in Glen Coe?", a: "<p>Glen Coe is notoriously wet. The valley funnels Atlantic weather systems and rainfall is among the highest in Britain. Pack waterproofs and be ready to appreciate the valley's moody beauty when the clouds descend, which they often do even in summer.</p>" },
        { q: "What day rides work well from Fort William?", a: "<p>The A830 Road to the Isles runs to Mallaig past the Glenfinnan Viaduct, Glen Nevis is a wild dead-end beneath Ben Nevis, and the Corran Ferry gives quick access to the Ardnamurchan peninsula and Britain's most westerly mainland point. The A86 to Spean Bridge is a fast run through the Great Glen.</p>" }
      ]
    },
    "applecross-bealach-na-ba-guide": {
      updatedDate: "2026-07-21",
      keyTakeaways: [
        "The Bealach na Ba rises from sea level at Kishorn to 626 metres, with Alpine-style hairpins, 20% gradients and cattle grids.",
        "Ride east to west (climbing from Kishorn) for the most dramatic ascent and stunning Skye views on the descent.",
        "There is no fuel in Applecross; fill up at Kishorn, Lochcarron or Shieldaig before crossing the pass.",
        "The Applecross Inn is famous across the Highlands for hand-dived scallops, Applecross Bay prawns and local venison.",
        "The pass can close in winter snow, so check conditions before attempting a crossing from November to March."
      ],
      comparisonTable: {
        caption: "Riding the Bealach na Ba: key facts",
        headers: ["Detail", "Info"],
        rows: [
          ["Summit height", "626 metres from sea level at Kishorn"],
          ["Best direction", "East to west for the dramatic ascent"],
          ["Gradient", "Up to 20% with hairpin bends"],
          ["Road width", "Single-track with passing places throughout"],
          ["Nearest fuel", "Kishorn, Lochcarron or Shieldaig"],
          ["Winter access", "Can close in snow, November to March"]
        ]
      },
      faq: [
        { q: "Which direction should I ride the Bealach na Ba?", a: "<p>Ride east to west, climbing from Kishorn, for the most dramatic ascent and the finest reward. The descent towards Applecross opens up views across the Inner Sound to Raasay and Skye, and on clear days the Outer Hebrides appear on the western horizon. It is the single greatest motorcycle road in Britain.</p>" },
        { q: "Is there fuel in Applecross?", a: "<p>No. There is no fuel station in Applecross, so fill up at Kishorn, Lochcarron or Shieldaig before crossing the Bealach na Ba. Mobile phone signal is also unreliable throughout the peninsula, so plan ahead and do not rely on your phone for navigation or emergencies.</p>" },
        { q: "Where can I eat and stay in Applecross?", a: "<p>The Applecross Inn is famous across the Highlands for its seafood, including hand-dived scallops, Applecross Bay prawns and local venison, and it has rooms. The Applecross Campsite is popular with motorcyclists and offers stunning sea views across to Skye.</p>" },
        { q: "Is there an alternative route to Applecross?", a: "<p>Yes. The coast road from Shieldaig runs along the northern shore of the peninsula through tiny settlements like Kenmore and Toscaig. It is slower and less dramatic than the Bealach, but the coastal views are beautiful and the sense of remoteness is profound. It is also the sensible option when the pass is closed by snow.</p>" },
        { q: "Can I ride the Bealach na Ba in winter?", a: "<p>Take great care. The Bealach na Ba can be closed by winter snow, so check conditions before attempting the crossing between November and March. Cattle grids on the pass become slippery when wet, and loose gravel can gather on the bends at any time of year.</p>" }
      ]
    },
    "torridon-wester-ross-guide": {
      updatedDate: "2026-07-21",
      keyTakeaways: [
        "Torridon's ancient sandstone mountains, Liathach and Beinn Eighe, are among the oldest in the world at around 750 million years.",
        "The A896 through Glen Torridon is narrow and atmospheric, with Liathach looming overhead the entire way.",
        "The A832 past Loch Maree is one of Scotland's most beautiful drives, alongside its finest freshwater loch.",
        "Explore dead-end single-track adventures like the B8056 to Red Point beach and the precipitous road to Diabaig.",
        "Traffic is minimal, but roads are largely single-track with passing places, so ride at an unhurried pace."
      ],
      comparisonTable: {
        caption: "Key roads in Torridon and Wester Ross",
        headers: ["Road", "Highlight", "Notes"],
        rows: [
          ["A896 through Glen Torridon", "Liathach looming overhead", "Narrow and atmospheric main route"],
          ["A832 Gairloch to Kinlochewe", "Loch Maree, Scotland's finest loch", "Flowing road with beautiful views"],
          ["B8056 to Red Point", "Stunning sandy beach and Skye views", "Dead-end road, retrace your route"],
          ["Minor road to Diabaig", "Remote fishing village", "Precipitous, not for the faint-hearted"]
        ]
      },
      faq: [
        { q: "What makes Torridon's mountains special?", a: "<p>Liathach and Beinn Eighe dominate the landscape and are among the oldest mountains in the world at around 750 million years. Liathach's ridgeline of pinnacles and terraces is visible from the road through Glen Torridon, while Beinn Eighe, with its white quartzite caps, is the centrepiece of Britain's oldest national nature reserve.</p>" },
        { q: "What are the best roads to ride here?", a: "<p>The A896 through Glen Torridon is the atmospheric main route with Liathach overhead, while the A832 from Gairloch to Kinlochewe runs past the beautiful Loch Maree. For adventure, the B8056 to Red Point ends at a sandy beach with Skye views, and the minor road to Diabaig is a precipitous single-track descent to a remote fishing village.</p>" },
        { q: "How busy are the roads in Wester Ross?", a: "<p>Traffic is minimal, which is one of the region's great pleasures. Most roads are single-track with passing places, so the pace is naturally slow, which suits scenery this good. Pull into passing places on your left and be prepared to reverse for oncoming vehicles on the tighter sections.</p>" },
        { q: "When is the best time to visit Torridon?", a: "<p>Late spring to early autumn offers the most reliable riding, with the longest days from May to July. Midges can be troublesome from June to August in still conditions, so carry repellent. The west coast sees significant rainfall, so waterproofs are essential year-round.</p>" },
        { q: "Where does Torridon fit into a longer tour?", a: "<p>Torridon links naturally with Applecross and the wider NC500. Many riders combine the Bealach na Ba, the Shieldaig coast road and Glen Torridon into a single spectacular loop, continuing north through Gairloch towards Ullapool for a multi-day west coast adventure.</p>" }
      ]
    },
    "outer-hebrides-motorcycle-guide": {
      updatedDate: "2026-07-21",
      keyTakeaways: [
        "The Outer Hebrides form a 130-mile island chain reached by CalMac ferries; book motorcycle spaces well in advance for summer.",
        "Luskentyre Beach on Harris is regularly voted one of the world's best, with turquoise water and white sand.",
        "The Callanish Standing Stones on Lewis are older than Stonehenge and set in the oldest rock in Britain, Lewisian gneiss.",
        "Fuel is available in Stornoway, Tarbert, Lochmaddy and Castlebay; elsewhere it is scarce, and many shops close on Sundays.",
        "Wind is a constant factor and can be strong enough to affect your riding across the open, treeless landscape."
      ],
      comparisonTable: {
        caption: "CalMac ferry routes to the Outer Hebrides",
        headers: ["Crossing", "Duration", "Notes"],
        rows: [
          ["Ullapool to Stornoway (Lewis)", "2 hours 45 minutes", "The most popular crossing"],
          ["Uig (Skye) to Tarbert (Harris)", "1 hour 40 minutes", "Connects with Skye"],
          ["Uig (Skye) to Lochmaddy (North Uist)", "1 hour 40 minutes", "Southern Skye link"],
          ["Oban to Castlebay (Barra)", "4 hours 50 minutes", "Southern entry point"]
        ]
      },
      faq: [
        { q: "How do I get to the Outer Hebrides with my motorcycle?", a: "<p>CalMac ferries connect the islands with the mainland. The most popular crossing is Ullapool to Stornoway on Lewis, taking 2 hours 45 minutes. From Skye you can sail from Uig to either Tarbert on Harris or Lochmaddy on North Uist, and the southern entry point is Oban to Castlebay on Barra. Book motorcycle spaces well in advance for summer, as they sell out quickly.</p>" },
        { q: "What are the highlights of Lewis and Harris?", a: "<p>On Lewis, the Callanish Standing Stones are older than Stonehenge and set on the oldest rock in Britain. Harris is famous for its extraordinary beaches, with Luskentyre regularly voted among the world's best. The twisting single-track Golden Road on Harris's east coast is a rewarding ride through a lunar landscape.</p>" },
        { q: "Where can I get fuel and supplies?", a: "<p>Fuel is available in Stornoway, Tarbert, Lochmaddy and Castlebay, but elsewhere fuel stops are scarce, so never pass one with less than half a tank. Many islands observe the Sabbath, so fuel stations, shops and restaurants may be closed on Sundays, particularly on Lewis. Carry cash as not all establishments take cards.</p>" },
        { q: "How long do I need for the Outer Hebrides?", a: "<p>The islands are compact enough to explore Lewis and Harris in two to three days, with a further two days for the Uists and Barra. Causeways connect North Uist, Benbecula and South Uist in a 60-mile chain, so island-hopping by road is straightforward once you have arrived.</p>" },
        { q: "What is the riding like on the islands?", a: "<p>Hebridean roads are generally quiet single-track with passing places, and traffic is light outside Stornoway. The scenery is vast and open, with Atlantic horizons and machair meadows. Wind is a constant factor and can be strong enough to affect your riding, while weather can alternate between rain, wind and sunshine within an hour.</p>" }
      ]
    },
    "orkney-islands-motorcycle-guide": {
      updatedDate: "2026-07-21",
      keyTakeaways: [
        "Orkney sits off Scotland's north coast across the Pentland Firth, with a mild Gulf Stream climate despite its northern latitude.",
        "The Heart of Neolithic Orkney is a UNESCO World Heritage Site, including Skara Brae, the Ring of Brodgar and Maeshowe.",
        "The Scrabster-Stromness and Gills Bay-St Margaret's Hope ferries suit NC500 riders adding Orkney as an extension.",
        "The Churchill Barriers, WWII causeways protecting Scapa Flow, connect the Mainland to South Ronaldsay for unique riding.",
        "Fuel and facilities are in Kirkwall and Stromness; allow two to three days for the Mainland plus a day per outer island."
      ],
      comparisonTable: {
        caption: "Ferry options to Orkney",
        headers: ["Operator", "Route", "Duration"],
        rows: [
          ["NorthLink Ferries", "Scrabster to Stromness", "90 minutes"],
          ["NorthLink Ferries", "Aberdeen to Kirkwall", "6 hours"],
          ["Pentland Ferries", "Gills Bay to St Margaret's Hope", "1 hour"]
        ]
      },
      faq: [
        { q: "Which ferry should I take to Orkney?", a: "<p>Two operators serve the islands. NorthLink runs Scrabster to Stromness in 90 minutes and Aberdeen to Kirkwall in six hours, while Pentland Ferries runs the quickest crossing from Gills Bay to St Margaret's Hope in one hour. The Scrabster and Gills Bay routes work best for NC500 riders adding Orkney as an extension.</p>" },
        { q: "What are the must-see sites on Orkney?", a: "<p>The Heart of Neolithic Orkney is a UNESCO World Heritage Site comprising Skara Brae, a 5,000-year-old village, the Ring of Brodgar stone circle, Maeshowe chambered cairn and the Standing Stones of Stenness. Scapa Flow and the moving Italian Chapel on Lamb Holm, built by wartime POWs, are also essential stops.</p>" },
        { q: "What is the riding like on Orkney?", a: "<p>Orkney's roads are well-maintained and mostly flat. The A960 and A964 form a circuit of the Mainland covering the main attractions in about 80 miles, with light traffic and frequent sea views. The Churchill Barriers, wartime causeways connecting the Mainland to South Ronaldsay, offer a unique riding experience.</p>" },
        { q: "How long should I spend on Orkney?", a: "<p>Allow two to three days for the Mainland and a further day for each outer island you visit. Inter-island ferries connect to smaller islands such as Hoy, with its Old Man of Hoy sea stack, and Rousay, the Egypt of the North. Taking your motorcycle on these ferries is straightforward and inexpensive.</p>" },
        { q: "What is the weather like?", a: "<p>Orkney is windy year-round, so dress accordingly and expect constant wind. Thanks to the Gulf Stream the islands are milder than you would expect for their latitude, but rain is frequent. The quality of light is extraordinary, particularly in the long summer days.</p>" }
      ]
    },
    "shetland-islands-motorcycle-guide": {
      updatedDate: "2026-07-21",
      keyTakeaways: [
        "Shetland lies closer to Norway than Edinburgh, with a distinctly Norse character and Britain's most northerly roads.",
        "NorthLink Ferries sails overnight from Aberdeen to Lerwick, a 12.5-hour crossing; book a cabin for the journey.",
        "The main A970 runs the length of the Mainland from Sumburgh Head to Toft, with roughly 500 miles of virtually empty roads.",
        "Reaching Unst, Britain's most northerly inhabited island, requires two short ferry crossings via Yell.",
        "Wind is Shetland's biggest challenge and is constant; pack warm, waterproof gear regardless of season."
      ],
      comparisonTable: {
        caption: "Must-see Shetland stops",
        headers: ["Stop", "Why Visit", "Notes"],
        rows: [
          ["Sumburgh Head", "Puffin colony with lighthouse", "Views to Fair Isle at the southern tip"],
          ["Jarlshof", "4,000 years of habitation", "One of Europe's finest archaeological sites"],
          ["St Ninian's Isle", "Britain's finest tombolo sand bar", "Site of a buried Viking treasure hoard"],
          ["Eshaness", "Volcanic sea cliffs", "Spectacularly dramatic in wind"],
          ["Unst", "Britain's most northerly island", "Muckle Flugga lighthouse, two ferries needed"]
        ]
      },
      faq: [
        { q: "How do I get to Shetland by motorcycle?", a: "<p>NorthLink Ferries operates an overnight sailing from Aberdeen to Lerwick, departing at 5pm and arriving at 7:30am. It is a proper 12.5-hour sea voyage across the North Sea, so book a cabin rather than sitting up all night. Alternatively, fly to Sumburgh Airport and hire a bike locally.</p>" },
        { q: "What is the riding like on Shetland?", a: "<p>Shetland's roads are well-maintained and virtually empty. The main A970 runs the length of the Mainland from Sumburgh Head to Toft, about 55 miles, and inter-island ferries connect to Yell, Unst, Whalsay and others. The total network is around 500 miles, enough for two to three full days of riding, with almost no traffic outside Lerwick.</p>" },
        { q: "How do I reach Unst, the most northerly island?", a: "<p>Reaching Unst requires two short ferry crossings, first from the Mainland to Yell and then from Yell to Unst. The reward is riding to the end of Britain, past the Hermaness National Nature Reserve to views of Muckle Flugga lighthouse and Out Stack, the UK's most northerly point.</p>" },
        { q: "Where can I get fuel and stay?", a: "<p>Lerwick has all facilities including fuel, supermarkets and accommodation. Elsewhere, fuel is available in Brae, Walls and Sandwick. Plan your fuel stops carefully given the distances and the ferry crossings between islands.</p>" },
        { q: "What should I expect from the weather?", a: "<p>Wind is Shetland's biggest challenge and is constant, sometimes very strong, so ride with care. Rain is frequent. The islands enjoy long summer days with nearly 24 hours of light in June, and dramatic northern lights in winter. Pack warm, waterproof gear regardless of season.</p>" }
      ]
    },
    "lake-district-destination-guide": {
      updatedDate: "2026-07-21",
      keyTakeaways: [
        "Choose your base by what you want to ride: Keswick for the northern lakes, Ambleside for the mountain passes, Penrith for value, Cockermouth for the quiet west.",
        "Ullswater is the most beautiful lake with the A592 along its shore; Wastwater is the deepest and most dramatic.",
        "Biker-friendly stops include the Kirkstone Pass Inn, the Old Dungeon Ghyll in Langdale and the Watermill Inn at Ings.",
        "May-June and September-October are the best months; July-August and bank holidays are extremely busy.",
        "Fuel is in Keswick, Ambleside, Penrith, Windermere and Cockermouth, but never on the mountain passes."
      ],
      comparisonTable: {
        caption: "Choosing your Lake District base",
        headers: ["Base", "Best For", "Notes"],
        rows: [
          ["Keswick", "Northern lakes", "Near Honister, Newlands and Whinlatter passes"],
          ["Ambleside", "Central passes", "Access to Kirkstone, Wrynose and Hardknott"],
          ["Penrith", "Eastern gateway", "Near Ullswater and the A66, good value"],
          ["Cockermouth", "Western lakes", "Quiet base near Buttermere and Ennerdale"]
        ]
      },
      faq: [
        { q: "Where should I base myself in the Lake District?", a: "<p>It depends on what you want to ride. Keswick suits the northern lakes and passes like Honister and Whinlatter, Ambleside gives access to Kirkstone, Wrynose and Hardknott, Penrith is a good-value eastern gateway near Ullswater, and Cockermouth is a quiet western base near Buttermere. The park is compact, so any base works for day rides.</p>" },
        { q: "When is the best time to ride the Lakes?", a: "<p>May-June and September-October are the best months, with quieter roads and peaceful fells. July and August are extremely busy, especially on the passes and around Windermere, and bank holiday weekends should be avoided entirely. Spring brings lambs and wildflowers while autumn brings golden fells and misty mornings.</p>" },
        { q: "Which pubs and cafes are biker-friendly?", a: "<p>The Kirkstone Pass Inn, one of England's highest pubs, is a classic mid-ride stop. The Old Dungeon Ghyll in Langdale is a legendary walkers' and bikers' pub, and the Watermill Inn at Ings near Windermere brews its own beer and has a biker-friendly car park. Keswick's Moot Hall is surrounded by good cafes.</p>" },
        { q: "Where can I get fuel?", a: "<p>Fuel is available in Keswick, Ambleside, Penrith, Windermere and Cockermouth, but there is no fuel on any of the mountain passes. Fill up in a town before tackling passes like Hardknott and Wrynose, which are steep, narrow and demanding.</p>" },
        { q: "What should I watch for when riding here?", a: "<p>Many Lake District roads carry a 40mph speed limit, so respect it. Rain is frequent and the western fells are among the wettest places in England, so carry waterproofs at all times. The mountain passes are steep with tight hairpins, so ride within your limits, especially in the wet.</p>" }
      ]
    },
    "yorkshire-villages-pubs-bikers": {
      updatedDate: "2026-07-21",
      keyTakeaways: [
        "Hawes is the Dales' capital and gateway to Buttertubs Pass, with the Green Dragon Inn and the Wensleydale Creamery.",
        "Reeth in Swaledale is near the Dales Bike Centre, which has a cafe, workshop and camping aimed specifically at motorcyclists.",
        "Helmsley is an elegant base for the North York Moors, with the Michelin-starred Star Inn at Harome two miles away.",
        "Yorkshire's real ales from Theakston, Black Sheep and Timothy Taylor are world-class, so plan accommodation or a pillion.",
        "Pub food is generally served 12-2pm and 6-9pm, and many Dales pubs close Monday or Tuesday lunchtimes."
      ],
      comparisonTable: {
        caption: "Yorkshire villages and their standout pubs",
        headers: ["Village", "Area", "Pub / Highlight"],
        rows: [
          ["Hawes", "Wensleydale", "Green Dragon Inn, Wensleydale Creamery"],
          ["Reeth", "Swaledale", "Buck Hotel, Dales Bike Centre nearby"],
          ["Malham", "Craven", "Lister Arms, near Malham Cove"],
          ["Helmsley", "North York Moors", "Star Inn at Harome (Michelin-starred)"],
          ["Goathland", "North York Moors", "Birch Hall Inn, Heartbeat filming location"]
        ]
      },
      faq: [
        { q: "Which Yorkshire village is best for motorcyclists?", a: "<p>Reeth in Swaledale is hard to beat thanks to the nearby Dales Bike Centre, which has a cafe, workshop and camping aimed specifically at motorcyclists. Hawes is the Dales' capital and gateway to Buttertubs Pass, with the Green Dragon Inn and the Wensleydale Creamery, and easy parking in the large market square.</p>" },
        { q: "Which pubs should I not miss?", a: "<p>The Tan Hill Inn is Britain's highest pub at 528 metres, remote and atmospheric. The tiny Birch Hall Inn at Goathland serves beer through a hatch, the Blue Lion at East Witton is superb for food, and the Falcon at Arncliffe was a filming location for the original Emmerdale. Yorkshire arguably does pubs better than anywhere in England.</p>" },
        { q: "When is food served in Yorkshire pubs?", a: "<p>Pub food is generally served between 12-2pm and 6-9pm, so arrive outside those windows and you may go hungry. Many Dales pubs also close on Monday or Tuesday lunchtimes, and popular pubs get busy at weekends, so it is worth booking for dinner, especially in summer.</p>" },
        { q: "Is parking easy in Yorkshire villages?", a: "<p>Most Yorkshire villages have free on-street parking suitable for motorcycles, and towns like Hawes and Helmsley have large market squares. Even where parking is tight, bikes can usually find a spot, making it easy to stop and explore between rides.</p>" },
        { q: "Which areas of Yorkshire are best for touring?", a: "<p>The Yorkshire Dales around Hawes, Reeth and Malham offer stone villages and classic pubs amid limestone scenery. The North York Moors around Helmsley and Goathland combine moorland riding with pretty villages, while the Wolds and East Yorkshire around Beverley and Stamford Bridge are quieter and often overlooked.</p>" }
      ]
    },
    "snowdonia-motorcycle-guide": {
      updatedDate: "2026-07-21",
      keyTakeaways: [
        "Snowdonia's mountain passes are the main draw, led by the dramatic Llanberis Pass (A4086) and Telford's Ogwen Valley (A5).",
        "The park is ringed by Edward I's iron ring of castles, including Caernarfon, Conwy, Harlech and Beaumaris.",
        "Betws-y-Coed is the outdoor capital of Wales and a well-equipped central base; Beddgelert is quieter and more intimate.",
        "Fuel is available in Betws-y-Coed, Beddgelert, Caernarfon and Dolgellau, and the A5 from Shrewsbury is the best approach from England.",
        "May to October is the best window, though the mountains attract cloud and rain, so waterproofs are essential year-round."
      ],
      comparisonTable: {
        caption: "Snowdonia's best mountain passes",
        headers: ["Pass", "Road", "Highlight"],
        rows: [
          ["Llanberis Pass", "A4086", "Sheer cliffs beneath Snowdon"],
          ["Ogwen Valley", "A5", "Glacial valley past Tryfan and Ogwen Falls"],
          ["Crimea Pass", "A470", "Fast flowing bends near Blaenau Ffestiniog"],
          ["Bwlch y Groes", "Minor road", "Wales' highest pass at 545 metres"]
        ]
      },
      faq: [
        { q: "Which are the best passes to ride in Snowdonia?", a: "<p>The Llanberis Pass on the A4086 is the most dramatic, running beneath Snowdon with sheer cliffs on both sides. The A5 through the Ogwen Valley is Telford's famous road through a spectacular glacial valley, the A470 Crimea Pass offers fast flowing bends, and Bwlch y Groes is Wales' highest pass at 545 metres.</p>" },
        { q: "Where should I base myself in Snowdonia?", a: "<p>Betws-y-Coed is the outdoor capital of Wales with excellent facilities and a central location. Beddgelert is a quieter, more intimate village at the foot of Snowdon, Dolgellau is the southern gateway near Cader Idris, and Caernarfon is a coastal base with a spectacular castle, good restaurants and nightlife.</p>" },
        { q: "When is the best time to visit?", a: "<p>Snowdonia is at its best from May to October. The mountains attract cloud and rain, so waterproofs are essential year-round. Summer brings long days and warmer temperatures but also tourist traffic on the passes, while autumn turns the oak woods golden and puts the first snow on the peaks.</p>" },
        { q: "Where can I get fuel, and what is the best approach road?", a: "<p>Fuel is available in Betws-y-Coed, Beddgelert, Caernarfon and Dolgellau. The A5 from Shrewsbury is the best approach road from England, itself a superb ride that leads directly into the heart of the park through the Ogwen Valley.</p>" },
        { q: "Are the roads safe to ride?", a: "<p>Mountain passes can be narrow with blind bends, so ride cautiously. Sheep roam freely on unfenced mountain roads and can appear without warning. Car parking charges apply at most mountain trailheads, and motorcycles usually pay the same rate as cars.</p>" }
      ]
    },
    "brecon-beacons-motorcycle-guide": {
      updatedDate: "2026-07-21",
      keyTakeaways: [
        "Now officially Bannau Brycheiniog, the park stretches from the Black Mountains in the east to the Black Mountain in the west.",
        "The A4069 Black Mountain Road is the undisputed highlight, climbing from Llandovery with sweeping bends and hairpins to Brynamman.",
        "The Gospel Pass above Hay-on-Wye is Wales' highest road at 549 metres, descending to the atmospheric Llanthony Priory.",
        "Waterfall Country around Ystradfellte has four major waterfalls, including Sgwd yr Eira where you can walk behind the water.",
        "Brecon, Abergavenny and Merthyr Tydfil all have fuel and services; May-September is the best riding window."
      ],
      comparisonTable: {
        caption: "Best routes in the Brecon Beacons",
        headers: ["Route", "Highlight", "Notes"],
        rows: [
          ["A4069 Black Mountain", "Signature flowing bends across moorland", "From Llandovery to Brynamman"],
          ["A470 through the Beacons", "The park's dramatic spine road", "Central mountain scenery"],
          ["Gospel Pass", "Wales' highest road at 549 metres", "Descends to Llanthony Priory"],
          ["A4059 Aberdare to Brecon", "Climbs from the Valleys to the plateau", "Scenic ascent"]
        ]
      },
      faq: [
        { q: "What is the best road in the Brecon Beacons?", a: "<p>The A4069 Black Mountain Road is the undisputed highlight and one of Britain's finest motorcycle roads. Starting from Llandovery, it climbs across open moorland with perfectly cambered sweeping bends, then descends towards Brynamman with tight hairpins and views across the Towy Valley. It alone justifies a visit to the park.</p>" },
        { q: "What is the Gospel Pass?", a: "<p>The Gospel Pass above Hay-on-Wye is Wales' highest road at 549 metres and one of the country's best-kept secrets. This narrow, unfenced mountain road climbs through the Black Mountains with views across the English border, then descends to Llanthony Priory, a ruined medieval priory with a hotel built into the ruins.</p>" },
        { q: "Where can I get fuel and stay?", a: "<p>The main towns of Brecon, Abergavenny and Merthyr Tydfil all have fuel, accommodation and services. Hay-on-Wye, the town of bookshops on the eastern edge, makes a charming base. The park is easily reached from Cardiff in about an hour, Bristol in 1.5 hours and Birmingham in two hours.</p>" },
        { q: "When is the best time to ride here?", a: "<p>The best months are May to September, with June particularly good for wild flowers on the moorland. Weather is changeable, as the Beacons attract rainfall, especially on the western side, so carry waterproofs. Some routes, such as the Epynt military range road, are only open most weekends.</p>" },
        { q: "What else is worth seeing beyond the roads?", a: "<p>Waterfall Country around Ystradfellte in the southern Beacons has four major waterfalls flowing through wooded gorges, the most famous being Sgwd yr Eira, where you can walk behind the falling water. The approach roads through the Neath Valley are steep, wooded and atmospheric.</p>" }
      ]
    },
    "peak-district-villages-guide": {
      updatedDate: "2026-07-21",
      keyTakeaways: [
        "Bakewell is the Peak District's unofficial capital and a natural hub, famous for the Bakewell Pudding, with fuel and all services.",
        "Castleton sits at the head of the Hope Valley and is the gateway to Winnats Pass and Mam Tor.",
        "Edale, the start of the Pennine Way, feels remote despite being close to Sheffield and Manchester, with classic bikers' pubs.",
        "The White Peak (south and central) is pretty limestone country; the Dark Peak (north and east) is rugged gritstone moorland.",
        "Fuel is available in Bakewell, Buxton and Matlock, but not in smaller villages, so plan stops accordingly."
      ],
      comparisonTable: {
        caption: "Peak District villages worth a stop",
        headers: ["Village", "Highlight", "Notes"],
        rows: [
          ["Bakewell", "Bakewell Pudding, market hub", "Monday market day, fuel and services"],
          ["Castleton", "Gateway to Winnats Pass and Mam Tor", "George and Castle Hotels for food"],
          ["Edale", "Start of the Pennine Way", "Rambler Inn and Old Nags Head"],
          ["Eyam", "The plague village, moving history", "The Miners Arms is excellent"],
          ["Flash", "England's highest village at 463m", "Exposed moorland setting"]
        ]
      },
      faq: [
        { q: "Which village makes the best hub for a ride?", a: "<p>Bakewell is the Peak District's unofficial capital and a natural hub for motorcyclists. It straddles the River Wye, is famous for the Bakewell Pudding (not tart, as locals are particular), and has fuel and all services. Monday is market day, when the town buzzes. Parking is available in the main car park.</p>" },
        { q: "What is the difference between the White Peak and Dark Peak?", a: "<p>The White Peak in the south and central area is limestone country, with green dales, stone walls and picturesque villages. The Dark Peak in the north and east is gritstone country, with wild moorland, peat bogs and exposed ridges. White Peak villages tend to be prettier, while Dark Peak villages are more rugged and atmospheric.</p>" },
        { q: "Where can I get fuel?", a: "<p>Fuel is available in Bakewell, Buxton and Matlock, but not in the smaller villages, so plan your stops accordingly. Fill up before heading out onto the moorland roads or tackling passes like Winnats near Castleton.</p>" },
        { q: "Do I need to book pub meals?", a: "<p>Peak District pubs are popular, so book for dinner, especially at weekends. Classic stops include the Rambler Inn and Old Nags Head at Edale, the George and Castle Hotels at Castleton, and the Miners Arms at Eyam. The park has one of the highest concentrations of good pubs of any national park in England.</p>" },
        { q: "Are there any local events worth timing a ride around?", a: "<p>Several villages host annual well dressings, unique floral art displays, from May to September, which are worth seeing if your ride coincides. Bakewell's Monday market is another good reason to time your visit, though it makes the town busier.</p>" }
      ]
    },
    "cotswolds-motorcycle-destination-guide": {
      updatedDate: "2026-07-21",
      keyTakeaways: [
        "The Cotswolds is England's largest Area of Outstanding Natural Beauty, stretching from Chipping Campden to Bath across six counties.",
        "Castle Combe is regularly voted England's prettiest village; Bibury has the famous 14th-century Arlington Row cottages.",
        "The B4632 escarpment road from Broadway to Cheltenham offers sweeping bends with panoramic views over the Vale of Evesham.",
        "The area is a food lover's paradise, from Daylesford Farm Shop to pubs like the Wheatsheaf in Northleach.",
        "Visit in spring (April-May) or autumn (September-October) to avoid severe summer congestion at Bourton-on-the-Water and Broadway."
      ],
      comparisonTable: {
        caption: "Cotswolds villages and roads",
        headers: ["Place", "Why Visit", "Notes"],
        rows: [
          ["Castle Combe", "Voted England's prettiest village", "Medieval cottages and market cross"],
          ["Bibury", "Arlington Row weavers' cottages", "14th-century terrace"],
          ["Broadway", "The Jewel of the Cotswolds", "Galleries and tearooms"],
          ["B4632 escarpment road", "Panoramic views over the Vale of Evesham", "Broadway to Cheltenham"],
          ["Snowshill", "National Trust manor of oddities", "Tiny, quiet hamlet"]
        ]
      },
      faq: [
        { q: "Which Cotswolds villages should I not miss?", a: "<p>Castle Combe is regularly voted England's prettiest village, a hidden valley of medieval stone cottages with a 14th-century market cross. Bibury has the famous Arlington Row, a terrace of 14th-century weavers' cottages, and Broadway, the Jewel of the Cotswolds, has a wide main street lined with galleries and tearooms. For quieter gems, try Snowshill, Naunton and Guiting Power.</p>" },
        { q: "What are the best roads for motorcycling?", a: "<p>The B4632 escarpment road from Broadway to Cheltenham offers sweeping bends with panoramic views east across the Vale of Evesham. The A44 from Moreton-in-Marsh to Chipping Norton flows through classic wold country, and the quiet lanes around the Slaughters and Bourton-on-the-Water follow the Windrush and Eye rivers.</p>" },
        { q: "When is the best time to visit the Cotswolds?", a: "<p>Visit in spring (April-May) or autumn (September-October) when the villages are quieter and the light is at its best. The area is busiest from June to August, with Bourton-on-the-Water and Broadway experiencing severe congestion on summer weekends, which spoils the relaxed riding the Cotswolds are known for.</p>" },
        { q: "Where should I eat and drink?", a: "<p>The Cotswolds is a food lover's paradise. Daylesford Farm Shop near Kingham is a temple of organic produce, and the pubs serve some of England's best food. Try the Wheatsheaf in Northleach, the Five Alls in Filkins or the Plough Inn at Cold Aston, washed down with local ales from Hook Norton or Donnington.</p>" },
        { q: "How accessible is the Cotswolds?", a: "<p>The Cotswolds is easily reached from London in about 90 minutes, Birmingham in an hour and Bristol in 45 minutes, making it ideal for a weekend break. Fuel is available in every market town. The riding is gentle and flowing rather than dramatic, proving that touring does not need mountain passes to be rewarding.</p>" }
      ]
    },
    "devon-coast-motorcycle-guide": {
      updatedDate: "2026-07-21",
      keyTakeaways: [
        "Devon has two contrasting coastlines: the rugged Atlantic north coast and the gentler, warmer south coast.",
        "The A39 Atlantic Highway follows the north coast from Barnstaple to the Cornish border through Ilfracombe and Lynmouth.",
        "The south coast around Dartmouth, Salcombe and the English Riviera has a Mediterranean feel and red sandstone cliffs.",
        "Dartmoor sits in the heart of Devon, and the B3212 across its spine adds a wild moorland detour to any coastal ride.",
        "Roads can be narrow with high hedges and blind bends, and fuel is scarce on Dartmoor between Moretonhampstead and Tavistock."
      ],
      comparisonTable: {
        caption: "Best Devon stops",
        headers: ["Stop", "Coast / Area", "Highlight"],
        rows: [
          ["Lynmouth", "North coast", "Dramatic harbour beneath towering cliffs"],
          ["Clovelly", "North coast", "Steep cobbled village, no cars"],
          ["Dartmouth", "South coast", "Naval town with castle and estuary views"],
          ["Salcombe", "South coast", "Yachting paradise, sandy beaches"],
          ["Widecombe-in-the-Moor", "Dartmoor", "Classic Dartmoor village and fair"]
        ]
      },
      faq: [
        { q: "What makes Devon a varied riding destination?", a: "<p>Devon is unique in having two dramatically different coastlines. The north coast is rugged and surf-battered, facing the Atlantic, while the south coast is gentler and warmer with palm-lined harbours and red sandstone cliffs. Between them lies Dartmoor, one of England's last true wilderness areas, so you can ride cliffs, moor and harbour in a single day.</p>" },
        { q: "What is the best road on the north coast?", a: "<p>The A39 Atlantic Highway follows the north coast from Barnstaple to the Cornish border, passing through Ilfracombe, Combe Martin and Hartland, swooping along the cliffs with views across the Bristol Channel to Wales. Lynmouth and Lynton, connected by a Victorian cliff railway, are the most dramatic coastal settlements.</p>" },
        { q: "Can I combine the coast with Dartmoor?", a: "<p>Yes, and it is well worth it. The B3212 from Exeter to Yelverton crosses the moor's spine, passing wild ponies, granite tors and medieval clapper bridges, with Two Bridges and Postbridge good stops for refreshments. The open, exposed moor is a dramatic contrast to the sheltered coast.</p>" },
        { q: "What should I watch for when riding in Devon?", a: "<p>Devon's roads can be narrow with high hedges and blind bends, so ride cautiously, especially on the south coast lanes. Fuel is available in all towns but scarce on Dartmoor between Moretonhampstead and Tavistock. Summer traffic can be heavy on the A38 and A380 approaches.</p>" },
        { q: "When is the best time to ride in Devon?", a: "<p>The best months are May-June and September, avoiding the peak summer crowds. Devon's climate is mild, rarely sees snow, and spring arrives weeks before the north, so the riding season is relatively long compared with the Highlands.</p>" }
      ]
    },
    "dorset-coast-destination-guide": {
      updatedDate: "2026-07-21",
      keyTakeaways: [
        "Dorset's Jurassic Coast is a World Heritage Site exposing 185 million years of history from Lyme Regis to Studland.",
        "The B3157 from Bridport to Weymouth follows the coast past Chesil Beach and the Isle of Portland.",
        "Corfe Castle is one of England's most dramatic ruins, guarding the only gap through the Purbeck Ridge.",
        "The Sandbanks Ferry connects Studland to Poole, taking motorcycles and avoiding a 25-mile detour around the harbour.",
        "The coast is busiest in July-August; visit in May-June or September for better riding conditions."
      ],
      comparisonTable: {
        caption: "Must-visit Dorset coastal stops",
        headers: ["Stop", "Why Visit", "Notes"],
        rows: [
          ["Lyme Regis", "Fossil capital, the Cobb harbour wall", "Excellent fish restaurants"],
          ["West Bay", "Dramatic sandstone cliffs", "Broadchurch filming location"],
          ["Lulworth Cove", "Perfect natural horseshoe bay", "Geological wonder"],
          ["Durdle Door", "Iconic natural limestone arch", "England's most photographed coast"],
          ["Corfe Castle", "Shattered Norman fortress on a hill", "Picture-perfect village beneath"]
        ]
      },
      faq: [
        { q: "What is the Jurassic Coast?", a: "<p>The Jurassic Coast is a World Heritage Site running from Lyme Regis to Studland, exposing 185 million years of Earth's history in cliffs, coves and beaches from the Triassic, Jurassic and Cretaceous periods. The B3157 from Bridport to Weymouth follows it closely, passing Chesil Beach and the Isle of Portland, with essential stops at Lulworth Cove and Durdle Door.</p>" },
        { q: "Should I use the Sandbanks Ferry?", a: "<p>Yes. The Sandbanks Ferry connects Studland to Poole and accepts motorcycles, running every few minutes. It avoids a 25-mile detour around Poole Harbour, saving considerable time and linking the Purbeck coast directly to the Poole and Bournemouth conurbation.</p>" },
        { q: "When is the best time to ride the Dorset coast?", a: "<p>The coast roads are busiest in July and August, so visit in May-June or September for better conditions and quieter roads. Dorset is easily reached from London in about 2.5 hours via the M3 and A31, Bristol in two hours and Southampton in one hour.</p>" },
        { q: "What is there to see inland?", a: "<p>Do not overlook inland Dorset. The Blackmore Vale is Thomas Hardy's Wessex, green farmland dotted with medieval churches and golden-stone villages. Sherborne has a magnificent abbey and castle, Cerne Abbas has the famous hillside giant, and the Dorset Downs offer gentle, flowing B-roads through rolling chalk landscape.</p>" },
        { q: "Where can I get fuel and food?", a: "<p>Fuel is available in all towns along the coast. Lyme Regis is renowned for its fish restaurants, and the working harbours at West Bay and Swanage offer fresh seafood. Corfe Castle village and the Purbeck towns provide plenty of pubs and cafes for a mid-ride stop.</p>" }
      ]
    },
    "scottish-highlands-hidden-gems": {
      updatedDate: "2026-07-21",
      keyTakeaways: [
        "The B8007 to Ardnamurchan Point reaches the most westerly point of the British mainland, accessed via the Corran Ferry from Fort William.",
        "Glen Affric, often called Scotland's most beautiful glen, penetrates deep into ancient Caledonian pine forest from Cannich.",
        "Many gems are on dead-end roads requiring you to retrace your route, so plan fuel and time carefully.",
        "The Black Isle near Inverness has Chanonry Point, one of Britain's best places to see bottlenose dolphins.",
        "Fuel is scarce and mobile signal non-existent in these remote areas, so carry supplies and share your plans."
      ],
      comparisonTable: {
        caption: "Highland hidden gems beyond the NC500",
        headers: ["Gem", "Highlight", "Access"],
        rows: [
          ["Ardnamurchan (B8007)", "Most westerly point of the mainland", "Corran Ferry from Fort William"],
          ["Glen Affric", "Scotland's most beautiful glen", "Road from Cannich, dead-end"],
          ["Knoydart", "Britain's last road-free wilderness", "Boat from Mallaig to Inverie"],
          ["Coigach", "Views to the Summer Isles", "Minor road from Achiltibuie"],
          ["Rannoch Moor", "Vast wild plateau", "B846 to Rannoch Station"]
        ]
      },
      faq: [
        { q: "Where is the most westerly point of mainland Britain?", a: "<p>It is Ardnamurchan Point, not Land's End. The single-track B8007 winds out to the lighthouse through a landscape of volcanic rock, Atlantic views and complete solitude. Access is via the Corran Ferry from Fort William, a short crossing that opens up one of the Highlands' most rewarding and least-visited peninsulas.</p>" },
        { q: "Which glen is worth the detour?", a: "<p>Glen Affric is often called Scotland's most beautiful glen. The road from Cannich penetrates deep into ancient Caledonian pine forest with views of Loch Affric and surrounding Munros. It ends at a car park, but the sense of entering a primordial landscape is powerful. It is a dead-end, so you retrace your route.</p>" },
        { q: "How should I prepare for these remote areas?", a: "<p>Many of these gems are on dead-end roads, so you will need to retrace your route and budget extra time. Fuel is scarce, so always fill up before exploring. Mobile signal is non-existent in most of these locations, so carry supplies and let someone know your plans if heading somewhere very remote.</p>" },
        { q: "Are any of these gems close to Inverness?", a: "<p>Yes. The Black Isle, which is actually a peninsula, sits just north of Inverness. Chanonry Point is one of the best places in Britain to see bottlenose dolphins, and the quiet farming villages and woodland walks make a peaceful contrast to the more dramatic Highlands further west.</p>" },
        { q: "Which of these can I reach without a boat?", a: "<p>Most, including Ardnamurchan (via the Corran Ferry), Glen Affric, Coigach, the Strathnaver Trail, the Monadhliath and Rannoch Moor via the B846 to Rannoch Station. Knoydart is the exception, being Britain's last road-free wilderness, reached by boat from Mallaig to Inverie, home to the remote Old Forge pub.</p>" }
      ]
    },
    "jersey-motorcycle-guide": {
      updatedDate: "2026-07-21",
      keyTakeaways: [
        "Jersey is the largest Channel Island at just 9 miles by 5, sitting closer to France than England.",
        "Condor Ferries run from Poole to St Helier, about 3 hours by high-speed craft, which accepts motorcycles; book early for summer.",
        "The north coast between Greve de Lecq and Rozel is the scenic highlight, with dramatic cliffs and hidden harbours.",
        "The Jersey War Tunnels, an underground hospital built by slave labour, are the island's most important historical site.",
        "The speed limit is 40mph maximum, dropping to 15mph on the Green Lanes where pedestrians and horses have priority."
      ],
      comparisonTable: {
        caption: "Jersey quick facts",
        headers: ["Detail", "Info"],
        rows: [
          ["Getting there", "Condor Ferries, Poole to St Helier, about 3 hours"],
          ["Speed limit", "40mph maximum, 15mph on Green Lanes"],
          ["Scenic highlight", "North coast, Greve de Lecq to Rozel"],
          ["Top history site", "Jersey War Tunnels (underground hospital)"],
          ["Currency", "Jersey Pound, interchangeable with Sterling"],
          ["Suggested time", "2 to 3 days"]
        ]
      },
      faq: [
        { q: "How do I get to Jersey with my motorcycle?", a: "<p>Condor Ferries operate from Poole to St Helier, taking about 4.5 hours on the conventional ferry or 3 hours by high-speed craft, which accepts motorcycles. Book well in advance for summer, as capacity is limited. Alternatively, fly into Jersey Airport with an airline such as Blue Islands and hire a bike locally.</p>" },
        { q: "What is the scenic highlight of Jersey?", a: "<p>The north coast is the island's scenic highlight. The cliffs between Greve de Lecq and Rozel are spectacular, with headlands dropping hundreds of feet to the sea and frequent viewpoints along the coast road. Stop at Devil's Hole, a dramatic sea-carved blow hole, and the hidden harbours of Bonne Nuit Bay and Bouley Bay.</p>" },
        { q: "What are the Green Lanes?", a: "<p>Jersey's interior is a maze of Green Lanes, narrow lanes where pedestrians, cyclists and horses have priority and the speed limit is just 15mph. They connect farms, manors and medieval churches through a patchwork of small fields. On a motorcycle they are charming and intimate, a world away from normal road riding.</p>" },
        { q: "What should I know about speed limits and fuel?", a: "<p>The maximum speed limit on Jersey is 40mph, dropping to 15mph on the Green Lanes, so this is a destination for relaxed exploration rather than fast riding. Fuel is cheaper than on the UK mainland, and the currency is the Jersey Pound, which is interchangeable with Sterling.</p>" },
        { q: "How long do I need, and what should I see?", a: "<p>Allow 2 to 3 days to explore Jersey properly. The Jersey War Tunnels, an underground hospital carved by slave labour during the wartime occupation, are the most important historical site. Also visit Mont Orgueil Castle above Gorey, La Corbiere Lighthouse and Elizabeth Castle in St Aubin's Bay.</p>" }
      ]
    },
    "guernsey-motorcycle-guide": {
      updatedDate: "2026-07-21",
      keyTakeaways: [
        "Guernsey is smaller than Jersey at just 25 square miles but arguably more characterful, with a strong independent identity.",
        "Condor Ferries run from Poole to St Peter Port, about 3 hours by high-speed craft; book motorcycle spaces in advance.",
        "The rugged south coast from St Peter Port to Pleinmont has the most dramatic scenery, with cliffs, caves and rocky inlets.",
        "St Peter Port is one of the prettiest harbour towns in the British Isles, with Castle Cornet and Victor Hugo's house.",
        "The maximum speed limit is 35mph, dropping to 15mph on the Ruettes Tranquilles quiet lanes."
      ],
      comparisonTable: {
        caption: "Guernsey quick facts",
        headers: ["Detail", "Info"],
        rows: [
          ["Getting there", "Condor Ferries, Poole to St Peter Port, about 3 hours"],
          ["Speed limit", "35mph maximum, 15mph on Ruettes Tranquilles"],
          ["Scenic highlight", "South coast, St Peter Port to Pleinmont"],
          ["Capital", "St Peter Port with Castle Cornet"],
          ["Currency", "Guernsey Pound, interchangeable with Sterling"],
          ["Suggested time", "1 to 3 days"]
        ]
      },
      faq: [
        { q: "How do I get to Guernsey by motorcycle?", a: "<p>Condor Ferries run from Poole to St Peter Port in about 3 hours by high-speed craft, with a slower but often cheaper conventional ferry available too. Motorcycle spaces should be booked in advance. The ferry arrives at St Peter Port, Guernsey's charming capital and the natural starting point for exploring the island.</p>" },
        { q: "What is the best part of Guernsey to ride?", a: "<p>The south coast is spectacularly rugged, with high cliffs, sea caves and rocky inlets. The coast road from St Peter Port west to Pleinmont passes some of the most dramatic scenery in the Channel Islands. Stop at Jerbourg Point for views to Jersey and the French coast, and Le Gouffre for a dramatic cliff-top walk.</p>" },
        { q: "What are the Ruettes Tranquilles?", a: "<p>Like Jersey's Green Lanes, Guernsey has a network of Ruettes Tranquilles, quiet lanes where the speed limit is 15mph and non-motorised traffic has priority. These lanes weave between granite farmhouses, greenhouses and ancient parish churches, and they are the soul of the island.</p>" },
        { q: "What are the speed limits and where is fuel?", a: "<p>The maximum speed limit is 35mph, dropping to 25mph in some areas and 15mph on the Ruettes Tranquilles, so leisurely riding is the order of the day. Fuel is available in St Peter Port, St Sampson's and dotted around the island, and it is cheaper than on the UK mainland. The Guernsey Pound is interchangeable with Sterling.</p>" },
        { q: "How long should I spend on Guernsey?", a: "<p>One to two days is enough to explore, but three days allows a relaxed pace. Do not miss St Peter Port, one of the prettiest harbour towns in the British Isles, with Castle Cornet guarding the harbour and Victor Hugo's house open for tours. Seafood is outstanding, so try the traditional Guernsey Bean Jar.</p>" }
      ]
    },
    "isle-of-man-beyond-tt": {
      updatedDate: "2026-07-21",
      keyTakeaways: [
        "The Isle of Man offers far more than the TT, with Viking history, mountain roads and no national speed limit on many derestricted roads.",
        "The Mountain Road from Ramsey to Douglas climbs to 422 metres and is derestricted outside race periods; ride responsibly.",
        "The Isle of Man Steam Packet sails from Heysham (3.5 hours), Liverpool (2.5 hours by fast craft) and Belfast.",
        "TT fortnight (late May/early June) has electric atmosphere but triple accommodation prices; ride the roads in April-May or September-October.",
        "Fuel is in every town and the Manx Pound is interchangeable with Sterling, with good mobile coverage across the island."
      ],
      comparisonTable: {
        caption: "Isle of Man riding beyond the TT course",
        headers: ["Route", "Highlight", "Notes"],
        rows: [
          ["Mountain Road", "Derestricted road to 422 metres", "See Scotland, England, Ireland and Wales"],
          ["The Coastal Road", "100-mile loop of the island", "About 3 hours of stunning coastline"],
          ["Marine Drive", "Cliff-top road, Douglas to Port Soderick", "Dramatic and scenic"],
          ["Millennium Way", "Heritage trail through the interior", "Castletown to Ramsey"]
        ]
      },
      faq: [
        { q: "How do I get to the Isle of Man with my bike?", a: "<p>The Isle of Man Steam Packet operates ferries from Heysham (3.5 hours), Liverpool (2.5 hours by fast craft) and Belfast. Book motorcycle spaces well in advance for the TT period, as they sell out months ahead. The island has fuel stations in every town and the Manx Pound is interchangeable with Sterling.</p>" },
        { q: "Is the Isle of Man worth visiting outside the TT?", a: "<p>Absolutely. Visit outside TT fortnight and you will find a peaceful, beautiful island with empty roads and a genuine motorcycle-friendly culture year-round. There is Viking history at Tynwald and the Manx Museum, medieval castles at Castletown and Peel, and stunning mountain and coastal roads to enjoy at your own pace.</p>" },
        { q: "What is the Mountain Road really like?", a: "<p>The Mountain Road from Ramsey to Douglas is the most spectacular section of the TT course, climbing to 422 metres across open moorland. On clear days you can see Scotland, England, Ireland and Wales at once. It is derestricted with no speed limit outside race periods, but it is a regular public road, so ride responsibly and within your abilities.</p>" },
        { q: "When is the best time to visit?", a: "<p>TT fortnight in late May and early June has electric atmosphere but accommodation prices triple and the course closes for practice and racing. The Manx Grand Prix in late August is smaller and more accessible. To ride the roads freely at your own pace, visit in April-May or September-October when the weather is reasonable and the roads are quiet.</p>" },
        { q: "What do the derestricted roads mean for riders?", a: "<p>Many Manx roads have no speed limit on derestricted sections, marked by the national speed limit sign. This reflects the island's motorcycle-friendly culture, not an invitation to race. These are regular public roads with junctions, pedestrians and oncoming traffic, so ride within your abilities and respect other road users.</p>" }
      ]
    }
  };
  ARTICLES.forEach(function(a) { if (ENRICH[a.slug]) { for (var k in ENRICH[a.slug]) a[k] = ENRICH[a.slug][k]; } });
}
